          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-4266b8d63fd3c12f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":3,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Natasha directly avows her love for her mother, strongly supporting T. The exclamative construction “как я вас люблю” (“how I love you”), erupting amid her sudden overflow of happiness, also gives the words an independently expressive/discharge-like function, supporting E beyond merely passionate delivery. Nothing indicates a relational undertaking or commitment, so P is unsupported.\",\"ambiguity\":\"E could be scored slightly lower if the line is read as an emphatic maternal-love avowal whose spontaneity and intensity are wholly contextual; its exclamative syntax and immediate affective overflow favor a substantial E reading.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Natasha directly addresses her mother with the exclamative avowal “как я вас люблю” while embracing her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Natasha’s joy concerns her apparent romantic future with Prince Andrei, but it overflows into an affectionate exclamation addressed to her mother. The maternal declaration is sincere as presented and functions both as an avowal of love and as an immediate verbal release of happiness and agitation; it does not undertake a new obligation or redefine their relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Голубушка, мамаша, как я вас люблю, как мне хорошо!” (“My darling Mama, how I love you, how happy I am!”)\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense love avowal supports T; the degree-exclamative form and its pairing with an exclamation of happiness support independent expressive force E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Natasha “крикнула” the words while “плача слезами счастья и волнения и обнимая мать” (crying tears of happiness and agitation and embracing her mother).\",\"supports\":\"The utterance occurs as an immediate affective outpouring. These delivery features reinforce, but do not alone establish, the E interpretation.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Natasha discusses Prince Andrei, fate, and marriage; her mother answers, “Les mariages se font dans les cieux.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration to her mother is prompted by an abrupt surge of romantic hope and relief, explaining its reflex-like expressive function without turning it into a commitment to the mother.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and E naturally capture the two salient forces: a sincere maternal-love avowal and an affect-driven exclamation. Context accounts for the romantic trigger and intense delivery; no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
